  "verbalization": "Methyl Lucidenate Q appears to interact with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase, a protein that is targeted by multiple complex molecules, including (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol and (2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-2-[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6S)-5-Hydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-2-[(4S,5'R,7S,8R,9S,13S)-5',7,9,13-tetramethylspiro[5-oxapentacyclo[10.8.0.02,9.04,8.013,18]icosane-6,2'-oxane]-16-yl]-2,3-bis[[(2S,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-trihydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxan-2-yl]oxy]oxan-4-yl]oxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol. These molecules are all involved in the metabolic pathway of sialic acid synthesis, and their interaction with CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase suggests a potential role in regulating sialylation. Additionally, the enzyme CMP-N-acetylneuraminate-beta-1,4-galactoside alpha-2,3-sialyltransferase is also targeted by Fructose 1,6-Diphosphate, which is a key intermediate in glycolysis. This indicates that Methyl Lucidenate Q may influence metabolic pathways by modulating the activity of this enzyme, potentially affecting sialylation and downstream metabolic processes. The evidence suggests a complex, multi-step relationship between Methyl Lucidenate Q and Hs746T, involving both enzymatic and metabolic interactions.",
